<p class="sst-lead">Inside an advanced semiconductor package, x-ray CT (xCT) reveals solder bumps, through-silicon
vias, and interfaces &mdash; each a boundary curve we can segment and feed to SST.</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>This is <strong>Challenge 2</strong> made concrete: take two views of a package &mdash; an <em>up</em> chip and
  a <em>down</em> chip, or a design versus its scan &mdash; and ask</p>
  <blockquote>Are these boundaries, <em>up to rigid motions</em>, the <strong>same</strong>? If not, is it
  <strong>scale</strong> or <strong>undulation</strong>?</blockquote>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p class="sst-note">NIST-CHIPS context: pairing metrology (xCT, measured), simulation (modeled scans), and CAD
  (design intent) lets us separate harmless <em>artifacts</em> of imaging from genuine <em>defects</em>.</p>
</div>
